> But wait a moment, are you sure that you have removed all the weird
> boot options as requested, and particularly iommu=3Dsoft?

> sudo cat /boot/loader/entries/linux.conf
> title Linux
> options root=3DUUID=3Da9c18677-7688-4fb4-a1e6-dd5fc4ee40a7 rw rootflags=
=3Dsubvol=3D/@ zswap.enabled=3D0 nowatchdog quiet thunderbolt.dyndbg=3D+p u=
sbcore.dyndbg=3D+p xhci_hcd.quirks=3D0x800000
> linux /vmlinuz-linux
> initrd /initramfs-linux.imgthunderbolt.dyndbg=3D+p usbcore.dyndbg=3D+p xh=
ci_hcd.quirks=3D0x800000

Yes, I am sure.

> Are you using some Thunderbolt adapter in a motherboard not officially
> supported by this adapter? I recall reading about such configurations
> that they may need various tweaks to work.

It is the ASUS TUF GAMING B650-PLUS WIFI motherboard paired with the
ASUS ThunderboltEX 4 expansion card. As far as I've been able to tell,
it should be supported. I've just double checked the motherboard's
manual to confirm. It says it supports USB4 devices, but it is not
very specific/enumerating devices. It definitely has all of the
required headers, advertises the proper protocol, and comes from the
same manufacturer.

> But Windows works, right?

Yes. When I boot into Windows, I have use of the USB ports on the dock
as well as displays routed through the dock at high resolution.

